<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  KM Admin's bundle manifest. `tools/dist/cmd.sh` substitutes @VERSION@ and copies this
  in; everything else is fixed.

  A fourth plist rather than a parameterised one, on the reasoning the other three already give:
  these are different *kinds* of thing, and four short manifests that each say what is true beat one
  with four conditions in it. This one declares no document type — it opens no files somebody
  double-clicks — and, like the remote's, it does talk to the local network.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>CFBundleName</key>
    <string>KM Admin</string>
    <key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
    <string>KM Admin</string>
    <!-- Distinct from the other three. Two bundles cannot share an identifier: LaunchServices keys
         its whole database on it, and a collision means one silently replaces the other. -->
    <key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
    <string>com.karaokemachine.admin</string>
    <key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
    <string>km-admin</string>
    <!-- Without extension. macOS looks for `<this>.icns` in Contents/Resources.

         This program's own mark: the theme's magenta, the same drawing the machine wears in amber,
         the builder in blue and the remote in green. Written by
         crates/playback/km-display/examples/icon.rs and staged by tools/dist/cmd.sh. This string is
         the file's name without its extension, so renaming icon/km-admin.icns means changing it
         here — and here only, as with the remote's. -->
    <key>CFBundleIconFile</key>
    <string>km-admin</string>
    <key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundleVersion</key>
    <string>@VERSION@</string>
    <key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
    <string>APPL</string>
    <key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
    <string>6.0</string>
    <key>LSMinimumSystemVersion</key>
    <string>11.0</string>
    <!-- Utilities, for the package builder's reason: it fetches soundfonts and wallpapers and
         plays none of them. -->
    <key>LSApplicationCategoryType</key>
    <string>public.app-category.utilities</string>
    <key>NSHighResolutionCapable</key>
    <true/>

    <!--
      **Two networks, and only one of them is this key's business.**

      This program reaches the internet — stock photograph APIs and the addresses SoundFont banks are
      published at — and macOS asks no permission for that. What it does ask about, since macOS 14, is
      the *local* network, and this program uses that too: the karaoke machine it sends files to is on
      the house network, named by address on its own page.

      Without this string the prompt is a bare request from a program somebody has just unzipped. With
      it, it says what the network is for. It does not remove the prompt and is not meant to.

      **Declining it leaves half the program working**, which is worth knowing before treating this as
      a prerequisite: searching, measuring, building a pack and downloading a bank all go out to the
      internet and are unaffected, and everything is written into this program's own folder either
      way. What stops working is sending any of it to the machine.
    -->
    <key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
    <string>KM Admin sends the pictures and instrument banks you choose to your karaoke machine on this network.</string>

    <!--
      **No NS*FolderUsageDescription keys, and no document type.** Like the remote and unlike the
      package builder, this never opens a file the user chose: everything it downloads, builds and
      remembers lives in its own data directory under ~/Library/Application Support, which no TCC
      grant covers. Declaring the Desktop, Documents and Downloads keys would make macOS prompt for
      access this program never uses.

      No microphone either — see the decision in docs/decisions/.
    -->
</dict>
</plist>
